<p>The cluster tells you what failed. But what actually happened on the wire?</p>
<p>Packet capture has long been the shared troubleshooting language for network, platform, application, and security teams. When logs, metrics, traces, and flow records do not fully explain a behavior, a capture often does. It is the record of what actually crossed the link, rather than what a counter believed had crossed it.</p>
<p>In the physical network the workflow was familiar. Capture traffic from a known point, export it, and open it in an analyzer. The capture point was a place you could name: a span port, a tap, a particular interface on a particular box that you could walk up to and touch.</p>
<p>In a cluster none of that holds. Workloads move between nodes, addresses are reused within seconds of being released, and the interface a process was using has often been torn down by the time anyone thinks to look at it. The question stops being "how do I capture from this interface" and becomes "how do I capture from this workload, wherever it happens to be running right now, and keep capturing when it moves".</p>
<p>That reframing is the whole design. A capture is expressed against a selector rather than an interface, the selector is evaluated continuously, and the resulting packets are labelled with the identity they came from rather than only the address. What comes back is a file an analyzer already understands, so the last mile of the workflow is unchanged.</p>
